Tinea pedis / Athletes Foot (chronic - fissuring)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Interdigital maceration/fissuring, Pruritic scaling, Moccasin distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-044
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe pain and inability to bear weight on the foot due to deep, infected fissuring between the toes.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with a long-standing foot rash that acutely developed a deep, agonizing fissure between the toes, causing severe pain, weeping, and a complete inability to wear shoes or bear weight. Urgent assessment was necessary to evaluate for secondary bacterial cellulitis entering through the fissured portal, achieve immediate symptom control, and initiate targeted antimicrobial and antifungal therapies before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The skin between my toes split wide open this morning. It's weeping, burning, and the pain is so sharp I can't even put my foot on the ground."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of intensely pruritic erythematous scaling between the toes, most prominently in the 4th/5th lateral web spaces. Over the last month the condition has been exacerbated by occlusive footwear and excessive sweating during back to back long work shifts, which has led to severe maceration and deep fissuring of the interdigital skin. The patient notes a diffuse fine scaling and hyperkeratosis on the soles (moccasin distribution). Today, the fissure in the 4th web space became deeply painful, erythematous, and began weeping serous fluid, accompanied by a distinct malodour. The acute mechanical pain with weight-bearing prompted the urgent call.
Functional Status: Temporarily functionally impaired; hobbling on the heel.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of rapidly progressing secondary bacterial cellulitis through the open fissure.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, sitting with the right foot elevated, wincing when the toes are manipulated.
Physical Exam: The right foot exhibits prominent, diffuse fine scaling and hyperkeratosis spanning the entire plantar surface and lateral aspects (classic moccasin distribution). In the 4th interdigital web space, the skin is starkly white, thickened, and severely macerated. A 1 cm deep, longitudinal fissure is present in the macerated tissue. It is highly erythematous at the base, actively weeping serous fluid, and exquisitely tender to light touch. There is a strong, foul malodour. Crucially, there is no spreading, hot cellulitic erythema ascending the dorsum of the foot, no purulent discharge, and no regional lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Heavy, unventilated work boots visible in the hallway.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Interdigital Tinea Pedis with fissuring and maceration - Status: Acute on Chronic Exacerbation
Differentials:

Bacterial Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The infection remains highly localized to the interdigital web space without the classic spreading, hot, demarcated erythematous plaque ascending the foot or leg, and the patient is afebrile.

Contact Dermatitis (Shoe allergy) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The specific localization deep in the interdigital webs and the presence of moccasin-type scaling on the soles, which are hallmarks of a dermatophyte infection rather than a contact allergy (which usually affects the dorsum of the foot).

Candidal Intertrigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of extensive moccasin-type scaling on the soles, which strongly favors dermatophyte (Tinea) over Candida (which typically remains confined strictly to the moist web spaces).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to risk of cellulitis)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute on chronic, severe exacerbation of long standing Tinea Pedis, resulting in a deep, painful interdigital fissure. Triage accurately flagged the sudden inability to bear weight and the open weeping wound as a high-acuity scenario requiring evaluation for secondary staphylococcal or streptococcal cellulitis. Immediate out-of-hours clinical assessment confirmed the absence of ascending cellulitis but recognized the deep fissure as a highly dangerous portal of entry. Immediate intervention with a combined topical antibacterial/antifungal agent, alongside strict drying instructions, rapidly addressed the acute pain, treated the fungal root cause, and definitively prevented an imminent bacterial superinfection that would require emergency hospitalization.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Cleansed the interdigital space with sterile saline and gently dried it. Prescribed a topical combination cream (Miconazole 2% / Hydrocortisone 1%) to be applied strictly to the painful fissure BD for 3 days to reduce acute inflammation and treat the fungus. Prescribed topical Terbinafine 1% cream to be applied to the entire sole and all web spaces daily for 2-4 weeks.
Positive Behavioral Change: By thoroughly drying the spaces between your toes with a tissue or cool hairdryer after every shower, you will eliminate the trapped moisture that the fungus needs to survive, preventing the skin from turning white and splitting open again. By switching to 100% cotton or moisture-wicking socks and changing them halfway through your work shift, you will keep the foot dry and create a hostile environment for the infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to ensure the fissure has closed and no cellulitis has developed.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the top of your foot suddenly becomes bright red, hot, and swollen, or if you develop a fever, uncontrollable shivering, or red streaks traveling up your leg, present immediately to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The deep split in the skin is a major risk for a serious bacterial infection. Strict adherence to treatment is required.
Clinical Course: The sharp pain and weeping from the fissure will improve dramatically within 48-72 hours. The thick scaling on the soles will require many weeks of continuous antifungal cream to fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor the top of your foot and ankle daily for any spreading redness or swelling.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A chronic fungal infection (athlete's foot) weakened the skin between your toes. The heavy sweating in your work boots caused the skin to become waterlogged and tear. The medicated creams will rapidly heal the tear and kill the fungus, but keeping the area bone-dry is the most important part of the cure.
Actionable Steps: Do not forcefully pull the toes apart. Apply the cream thinly. Treat your work boots with an antifungal powder to kill residual spores.

